# PENDING — what is not urgent, and must not be lost

**Status:** current · **Type:** entry-point · **Updated:** {{date}} · **Owner:** {{owner}}
**Scope:** ⚠️ INSTANCE document — it lists THIS installation's debt. It never travels.

---

## Purpose

Findings, debts and decisions that are **real but not blocking**. Without a home, these live in
somebody's head and die with the session that noticed them.

> ## ⭐ THE FAILURE THIS PREVENTS
> The most expensive thing lost in a session is not the code — it is **the thing someone noticed
> and did not have time to fix.** It gets rediscovered months later as an incident, and the second
> discovery costs far more than writing it down would have.

---

## ⛔ THE RULE THAT KEEPS THIS FILE ALIVE

> ## Rotate the FILE, not the item.

An item opened in January may still be open in December. What rotates is **the file that contains
it**, on a fixed period; the item is carried forward until it actually closes.

⛔ **A rotation that closes items by date is a rotation that hides debt.** The item does not stop
being true because a month ended — it just stops being visible, which is worse: now nobody knows
it exists and nobody decided to drop it.

⚠️ **Two lists of pending items always diverge.** If a second one appears anywhere, one of them is
already lying. Keep one, and point at it from everywhere else.

---

## The shape of an entry

```markdown
### <ID> · <one line saying what it is>

- **Priority:** 🔴 urgent · 🟠 medium · 🟢 no rush
- **Status:** open | paused | closed
- **Created:** <date> · **Updated:** <date> · **Closed:** —
- **Files:** where it lives, if it has a place yet
- **Depends on:** what has to happen first, or `—`

**Description.** What was found, and ⭐ **why it matters** — the consequence if it is never
addressed. A pending item with no stated consequence gets read as a preference and never
gets prioritised.
```

⭐ **The `Depends on` field is the one people skip and the one that saves the most time.** An item
that cannot move until something else happens is not "pending" — it is *waiting*, and mixing the
two makes the list look longer than the work actually is.

---

## What goes here, and what does not

| ✅ Belongs here | ⛔ Does not |
|---|---|
| something real you found and cannot fix now | work in progress — that is a block |
| a decision you are deliberately deferring | ⭐ a decision already taken — that is a decision record |
| debt a closed block did not resolve | an idea with no evidence behind it |
| an item waiting on someone else | anything urgent — ⚠️ if it is urgent it is not pending, it is the next step |

⚠️ **The line against a block:** a pending item is something *nobody is working on*. The moment
someone starts, it becomes a block and leaves this list. An item that stays here while being
worked on is how two people do the same thing twice.

